# Freshwater fish: SeaLife, Naturalist, and vanilla salmon.
#
# Row format:   group = spring summer autumn winter     (ordinary biomes)
#               group = dry wet                         (tropical biomes)
# The first row that fits wins. 'any' fits everything.
#
# What a number means for a fish. The game spawns fish in any water inside
# a biome, so "forest" here means a pond or river inside a forest. The
# number is how likely a pick goes ahead. I read it as how many of the
# species you would see in the water that season. Peak season is 1.0.
# The other seasons are cut.
#
# Fish do not leave. Cold-blooded fish slow down in cold water and sit in
# deep holes. They are still there under the ice. So winter is a low
# number and not zero, except where the fish cannot live at all.
#
# Weak spots, said once here:
#  - Most of the cuts are my size call. The sources give the direction
#    and the timing. They do not give a number.
#  - A fish in a deep hole is not "gone". The cut is about what you meet
#    in the shallows. It is a hack that treats seen and present as the same.
#  - Mangrove swamp counts as tropical in this pack. A temperate fish with
#    only 4-number rows is left at 1.0 there. I did that on purpose. Real
#    carp, bass and pickerel do live in warm southern swamps.
#  - Terralith ice_marsh, desert_oasis and warm_river are in some spawn
#    tags. I did not check which groups they land in. Run the audit.
#  - Ocean-going fish (electric ray, and the sea phase of salmon and
#    lamprey) are not here. See the docs fragment, 'Left alone'.

# ---- warm-water fish: slow in the cold, active when it warms ----

[bass]
animals    = sealife:bass naturalist:bass
confidence = sourced
source     = https://ncpedia.org/largemouth-bass-nc-wins
why        = Largemouth bass spawn in spring. Males move to the shallows as the water
             nears 60 F and nest when it holds 63 to 68 F for several days. In winter
             they get sluggish, eat little and sit deep. A warm spell over 50 F wakes
             them. So spring and summer are the busy half and winter is thin. The page
             does not say how thin. Habitat fix: bass do not live in snow country.
             Naturalist puts them in Terralith's ice marsh. That biome gets a
             small summer chance and nothing else. Both ids share one rule because
             both mods mean the same fish.
#            spring summer autumn winter
snowy = 0.0  0.3  0.1  0.0
any   = 0.9  1.0  0.7  0.2

[carp-koi-goldfish]
animals    = sealife:carp sealife:koi sealife:goldfish
confidence = reasoned
source     = https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC11576824/
             https://pondinformer.com/?p=39844
why        = All three are carp relatives. Common carp winter in deep water and move to
             shallow, weedy water in spring to spawn once it is about 13 to 18 C. They
             survive under ice. Pond goldfish and koi stop eating below about 10 C and
             sit near the bottom in a half-sleep. They eat the most in spring and
             autumn. I read these as search-result summaries. I did not open the pages.
             Koi and goldfish are copied from carp. Nobody studied them wild here.
#     spring summer autumn winter
any = 0.9  1.0  0.7  0.2

[catfish]
animals    = sealife:catfish naturalist:catfish
confidence = reasoned
source     = https://www.in-fisherman.com/editorial/the-spring-catfish-spawn-connection/155780
why        = Catfish gather in deep winter holes and stay put. They start to feed at
             55 to 60 F, move shallow in spring, and spawn in late spring and summer at
             70 to 85 F. So summer is the peak and winter the low. I read a search
             summary, not the page. Habitat fix: no catfish lives in an ice marsh.
             Naturalist spawns them there, so snowy is nearly shut.
#            spring summer autumn winter
snowy = 0.0  0.2  0.0  0.0
any   = 0.6  1.0  0.7  0.15

[bowfin]
animals    = sealife:bowfin
confidence = reasoned
source     = https://vtfishandwildlife.com/learn-more/vermont-critters/fish/bowfin
why        = Bowfin spawn in May and early June at 60 to 66 F. The male guards a nest
             in weedy shallows, which makes late spring the most visible time. The page
             says nothing about winter. The cold-season cut is the same as for other
             warm-water fish and is a guess. Bowfin can breathe air, so low oxygen
             in a swamp pond does not push them out.
#     spring summer autumn winter
any = 0.8  1.0  0.7  0.2

[pickerel]
animals    = sealife:pickerel
confidence = reasoned
source     = https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chain_pickerel
             https://www.maine.gov/ifw/docs/strategic-management-plans/pickerel.pdf
why        = Chain pickerel spawn late winter to early spring in flooded plants. A few
             southern ones spawn in winter and some populations have a small fall
             spawn. They are ambush fish that stay in weed beds and are fairly cold
             tolerant. So spring is the peak, summer and autumn are a little under
             it, and winter is cut. I did not open the pages.
#     spring summer autumn winter
any = 1.0  0.8  0.8  0.4

[minnow]
animals    = sealife:minnow
confidence = reasoned
source     = https://academics.cehd.umn.edu/hatch/research/fish/fishes/fathead_minnow.html
why        = Fathead minnows start spawning in late May to early June once the water is
             over about 16 C and keep going to mid-August. Spawning stops under about
             10 C. The cold half is quiet. The page is about the fathead only.
             SeaLife's minnow is a generic small fish, so this is the closest match.
#     spring summer autumn winter
any = 0.7  1.0  0.7  0.2

[chub]
animals    = sealife:chub
confidence = reasoned
source     = https://academics.cehd.umn.edu/hatch/research/fish/fishes/creek_chub.html
why        = Creek chub spawn from March to July depending on latitude, at 12 to 19 C.
             The male builds a gravel ridge in a stream with some current. That makes
             spring the busy season and a little into summer. Autumn and winter are
             quiet. I read search excerpts of the page, not the whole page.
#     spring summer autumn winter
any = 1.0  0.9  0.6  0.2

# ---- cold-water fish: comfortable in the cold, stressed or gone in summer heat ----

[trout]
animals    = sealife:trout
confidence = reasoned
source     = https://tacklevillage.com/when-do-brook-trout-spawn/
             https://www.ctriver.org/post/fall-redds
why        = Brook and brown trout spawn in autumn. Brook trout run roughly September to
             November and browns from mid-September into November. Eggs sit in gravel
             redds over winter. Autumn is when you see them, in the shallows over
             gravel. The summer dip is a guess. Trout are cold-water fish and
             warm summer streams push them to deep pools and springs. I did not search
             for that. The sources are a tackle site and a river group. Not strong.
#     spring summer autumn winter
any = 0.8  0.6  1.0  0.6

[pike]
animals    = sealife:northern_pike
confidence = sourced
source     = https://digital.outdoornebraska.gov/nebraskaland-magazine/ice-off-pikes-up
why        = Pike spawn as soon as the ice goes, in shallow weedy water and marshes.
             Nebraska Game and Parks says they can start under the ice if it lasts. So
             spring is the peak. The page does not cover summer or winter. They are
             a cold and cool-water fish, so I kept winter fairly high. Summer is cut
             because warm shallows push them to deeper water. That part is reasoned,
             not read. Their spawn list is all cold biomes (taiga, snowy, grove,
             frozen river), so no habitat fix is needed.
#     spring summer autumn winter
any = 1.0  0.7  0.8  0.6

[perch]
animals    = sealife:perch
confidence = sourced
source     = https://dnr.wisconsin.gov/topic/Fishing/species/yperch.html
why        = Yellow perch spawn in April or early May, soon after ice-out, at 45 to 52 F.
             The Wisconsin page also says they favour colder water and sit deeper. A
             Michigan DNR page that I could only see in a search summary says they stay
             active all winter under the ice. So the winter cut is mild. Spring is
             the visible peak.
#     spring summer autumn winter
any = 1.0  0.7  0.9  0.6

[walleye]
animals    = sealife:walleye
confidence = reasoned
source     = https://www.crescent-news.com/sports/outdoors/temperature-stream-flow-key-to-walleye-run/article_f34514c9-a8b2-500e-9f78-5e6c9560e515.html
             https://www.in-fisherman.com/editorial/hot-little-numbers-for-river-walleyes/154576
why        = Walleye run up rivers to spawn in early spring. On the Maumee the peak is
             the last week of March into early April at 40 to 50 F. Males come first
             when the water is barely above freezing. Rivers get the strongest spring.
             Elsewhere they are a cool-water fish that stays active under ice. Summer
             and winter cuts are my size call. Sources are a local paper and an angling
             magazine. I read search summaries only.
#         spring summer autumn winter
river = 1.0  0.6  0.7  0.5
any   = 0.9  0.7  0.8  0.6

# ---- fish that move between sea and river ----

[lamprey]
animals    = sealife:lamprey
confidence = sourced
source     = https://www.fws.gov/midwest/SeaLamprey/
why        = Sea lampreys run up streams in early spring and spawn in April. Males start
             a nest once the water is past about 4.5 C. In Portugal the run goes from
             December to June with a peak in February and March, so the date moves with
             latitude. Adults die after spawning. So a river is busy in spring and
             nearly empty by summer. The sea phase has no season, so only river rows
             exist. Ocean biomes stay at 1.0. The summer to winter numbers are my size
             call.
#          spring summer autumn winter
river = 1.0  0.2  0.2  0.4

[salmon]
animals    = minecraft:salmon
confidence = sourced
source     = https://trainingcenter.fws.gov/resources/knowledge-resources/salmon/asalmon3.html
why        = Atlantic salmon enter New England rivers from spring, peak in June, and
             spawn from late October through November. Pacific fall-run chinook and
             coho come in from late July and spawn October to December. I saw that in
             search summaries, not on a page I opened. Most adults die after spawning.
             So a river is fullest in autumn and thin in the dead of winter. Salmon
             at sea have no season in this data, so the cold oceans stay at 1.0 and
             only river and frozen river get rows. Vanilla also spawns salmon in
             frozen, cold and deep cold oceans.
#          spring summer autumn winter
river = 0.5  0.7  1.0  0.3

# ---- tropical and warm-country fish: dry / wet ----

[lungfish]
animals    = sealife:lungfish
confidence = sourced
source     = https://www.frontiersin.org/articles/10.3389/fphys.2016.00532/full
why        = African lungfish dig into mud and seal themselves in a mucus cocoon when
             their water dries up. They live on in there for months and sometimes years
             and wake within a day of the rain. A buried fish cannot be seen, so the
             dry half is mostly empty. The page gives no calendar months, only "the
             dry season". Dry and wet is the right axis for that. Cold swamp is a
             habitat fix. These are tropical fish and the temperate swamp has a
             real winter, so winter there is nearly shut. Not every lungfish
             sleeps in the dry. The Australian one does not. The mod does not say
             which kind this is. I assumed African.
#               dry  wet
jungle   = 0.2  1.0
swamp    = 0.3  1.0
#              spring summer autumn winter
swamp = 0.5  1.0  0.5  0.1

[pupfish]
animals    = sealife:pupfish
confidence = reasoned
source     = https://www.desertusa.com/fish/pupfish.html
why        = Desert pupfish go dormant in the cold, buried in mud, and come back as the
             water warms. Spawning starts at the end of February and runs through
             summer. Summer heat dries many pools and kills most fish. In this pack
             the desert and savanna have no cold season, only a wet and dry year.
             Winter dormancy does not apply there. I put the pool-drying idea on the
             dry half, but that is a guess. The page does not tie it to a dry
             season. River is a temperate biome here, and a desert fish in a cold
             river goes dormant in winter.
#               dry  wet
desert  = 0.6  1.0
savanna = 0.7  1.0
#           spring summer autumn winter
river = 0.9  1.0  0.7  0.1

[siamese-fighting-fish]
animals    = sealife:siamese_fighting_fish
confidence = guess
source     = https://seriouslyfish.com/species/betta-splendens
             https://sites.google.com/view/lotusfins/betta-fish/splenden-complex
why        = Wild bettas live in rice paddies, swamps and ditches in Thailand and
             nearby. Hobby pages say they spread across flooded land in the rainy
             season and get stuck in pools and ditches in the dry season. Neither page
             says when they breed. The dry cut is a guess built on that spread. The
             sources are fish-keeping sites, not field studies. Tropical fish
             cannot take a cold swamp, so plain swamp has a cold-weather cut.
#               dry  wet
jungle = 0.6  1.0
swamp  = 0.6  1.0
#           spring summer autumn winter
swamp = 0.7  1.0  0.7  0.1

[neon-tetra]
animals    = sealife:neon_tetra
confidence = guess
source     = none found
why        = I found no page on wild neon tetra seasons. They are South American
             blackwater fish and cannot take a cold winter. So the only rule is a
             habitat fix. A cold swamp in winter is nearly shut. Jungle and mangrove
             swamp stay at 1.0. A tetra study from Colombia suggests many tropical fish
             breed in the flood season, but that is a different species.
#           spring summer autumn winter
swamp = 0.7  1.0  0.7  0.1

[piranha]
animals    = sealife:piranha naturalist:piranha
confidence = guess
source     = https://doaj.org/article/32f34c405a0f41d3af255aa492da4830
why        = Red-bellied piranha breed twice a year, tied to the flood pulse. They
             spawn in flooded plants. In the dry season they crowd into the main
             channel and feed more. These two effects pull in opposite directions
             for a tropical biome, so jungle and mangrove swamp are left at 1.0.
             The only rule is a habitat fix. Piranha are warm-water fish and a cold
             swamp has no place for them. Winter there is shut and spring and
             autumn are cut hard. Lush caves, which Naturalist also uses, has no
             row and stays at 1.0. The cold numbers are a guess. I did not search
             for the piranha's cold limit.
#           spring summer autumn winter
swamp = 0.4  1.0  0.4  0.0
